From 54d78dc8ccd7758ebeb200beb42eacab52b39b97 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Faure <david.faure@kdab.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 17:39:15 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] QCoreApplication/Linux: revert the AT_EXECFN fast path

applicationFilePath() is expected to return a canonical path: it is what
applicationDirPath() makes plugin, translation and data paths relative
to, and qAppFileName() on macOS canonicalizes explicitly for that
reason. AT_EXECFN says how the program was invoked, not what was
invoked, so it keeps whatever symlinks the caller used. An application
started through a symlink by an absolute path - which is what gdb,
valgrind and IDEs do, they make the program path absolute but not
canonical - then looked for its files next to the symlink instead of
next to itself.

This is the same problem the absolute-path check in that commit was
added for: patch set 3 added it after a relative AT_EXECFN made
tst_selftests fail in CI. A symlink cannot be seen from the string,
though, so that check cannot cover it.

Canonicalizing AT_EXECFN afterwards is not cheaper, however it is done:
glibc's realpath() walks every component, and letting the kernel do it
by opening the file and reading /proc/self/fd costs an open() and a
readlink() where /proc/self/exe is one readlink of a path the kernel
has already resolved. Measured here (Linux 7.2, glibc 2.44):

    getauxval(AT_EXECFN)                          7 ns
    readlink("/proc/self/exe")                  530 ns
    realpath(AT_EXECFN), short path             344 ns
    realpath(AT_EXECFN), deep symlinked path   1898 ns

That leaves no fast path that is also correct. This does bring back
following a rename of the executable, which the reverted commit called
fragile and inconsistent with other OSes, but returning the right path
for the executable we are running matters more, and that is how Linux
behaved for years.

Amends 3d5ba3075ab0.

